<section>
  <h2>Explanation</h2>
  <p>
    A person of mass <i>m</i> rides in a lift that accelerates at <i>a</i>,
    with upward positive. Newton’s second law gives the cable tension, or
    the scale reading, <i>T</i> = <i>m</i>(<i>g</i> + <i>a</i>).
    <i>a</i> = 0 is at rest so <i>T</i> is the true weight;
    <i>a</i> = −<i>g</i> is free fall.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same equation covers a load hanging from the lift ceiling and a
    person standing on a scale. Both are the apparent weight.
  </p>
  <p>
    Taking upward as positive:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><i>T</i> − <i>mg</i> = <i>ma</i></li>
    <li>so <i>T</i> = <i>m</i>(<i>g</i> + <i>a</i>)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If the lift accelerates upward, <i>T</i> is greater than the weight. If
    it accelerates downward, <i>T</i> is smaller. If <i>a</i> ≤ −<i>g</i> the
    cable slacks and <i>T</i> = 0.
  </p>
</section>
